---
// Construction site cleaning — one of the THIRTEEN "by type of premises" leaf
// pages under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts, "By type of
// premises" group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster.
//
// ⚠️ SLUG. navigation.ts calls this "Construction site" and points at
// /construction-site/, which is the literal menu label rather than a phrase
// anyone searches. The head terms are "builders clean" and "construction
// cleaning". The slug is kept as-is because changing it means changing
// navigation.ts, this file and every cross-link in src/data/premises.ts
// together, and that is a decision for launch rather than for this build.
// The TITLE and the H1 lead with "builders clean" instead, which is where it
// actually matters for search.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT from its twelve siblings, structurally:
//   - The scope is a SEQUENCE, not a set. Three cleans happen at three
//     points in a build and they are different jobs, so §4 is
//     `ServiceBlocks variant="slider"` — one slide per stage, moved through
//     in order. The only use of that variant in the cluster, and the only
//     page whose content has an inherent order.
//   - `Callout tone="warning"` on silica dust, and it is the most serious
//     warning on the whole site. Not softened.
//   - `MetricsBlock variant="list"` is deliberately NOT used and no numbers
//     appear anywhere: this page has no honest figures and inventing a
//     "500+ sites cleaned" is exactly what the brand foundation document's
//     differentiator 2 argues against.
//   - No `ComparisonTable`. The shared rows are written for someone choosing
//     who enters their home; a site supervisor is choosing a subcontractor
//     who can hit a handover date. Deliberate omission, same call as strata.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - IS THIS SAFE FOR TLB TO OFFER? This is the one page in the cluster
//     where the answer might be no. A builders clean is construction work on
//     a construction site: white card induction, site-specific induction,
//     SWMS, silica exposure, working near live trades, and often working at
//     height. Confirm TLB has the tickets, the equipment and the insurance
//     before this page ships. If the honest answer is "we do the final
//     detail clean after handover but not builders cleans", then §4's first
//     two slides come off and this page becomes a much shorter one.
//   - Whether TLB holds a white card for every cleaner who would attend.
//     Bracketed in branded FAQ 1. Without it, no builder can lawfully let
//     them on site.
//   - Whether TLB has an H-class vacuum. Bracketed in §4 stage two. An
//     ordinary vacuum on construction dust is both useless and a health
//     issue, and this is the equipment question a builder will ask.
//   - Waste removal. Builders routinely expect the cleaner to take rubbish
//     away. Bracketed in §4 stage one.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import Callout from '../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import ImageBand from '../components/sections/ImageBand.astro';
import TagCloud from '../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import TestimonialCarousel from '../components/sections/TestimonialCarousel.astro';
import Faq from '../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
//
// ⚠️ THE HI-VIS EXCEPTION. IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 sets the canonical uniform as
// aqua polo, dark teal apron and teal gloves, and the site's palette rule
// forbids yellow. All three frames on this page break that rule on purpose:
// a cleaner on a construction site wears a high-visibility vest, and
// fluorescent lime-green is the standard Australian hi-vis colour and the
// closest real one to the brand's Mint Green. A frame of a cleaner in an
// apron on a live site would be a photo of something that must not happen.
// The apron is dropped for the same reason. Documented in the folder's
// IMAGE-PROMPTS.md so this does not get "corrected" back later.
import heroImg from '../assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/construction-cleaner-sweeping-a-new-build-before-handover.jpg';
import windowsImg from '../assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/construction-cleaner-detailing-new-window-frames.jpg';
import bathroomImg from '../assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/construction-final-detail-clean-of-a-new-bathroom.jpg';
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import definitionBgImg from '../assets/home/house-cleaning-cleaner-wiping-tall-interior-window.png';
import footerLogoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import type { TextBlockBody } from '../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../data/navigation';
import { nswLocationGroups } from '../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../data/premises';

// §0 Metadata. "Builders clean" leads the title because it is the head term,
// even though the slug is the menu's literal label. See the file header.
const pageMeta = {
  title: 'Builders Clean and Construction Site Cleaning | TLB Cleaning',
  description:
    'Builders cleans and final detail cleans for new builds and renovations across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed. Booked to your handover date, with dust taken seriously.',
  // ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.
  canonical: 'https://example.com/construction-site/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

// §1 Hero. `full-width-photo` — an empty new build is an establishing shot
// and the whole point of the frame is the space, same reasoning as the
// strata lobby. A half-width panel would crop the thing a builder is looking
// at, which is how finished the room is.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED, and note the hi-vis exception above.
const hero = {
  kicker: 'Builders clean and construction site cleaning',
  headingLines: ['The last trade on site', 'is the one they remember.'],
  lead:
    'Builders cleans and final detail cleans for new builds, renovations and fitouts across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed. Booked to your handover date, not to ours.',
  cta: walkthroughCta,
  image: {
    ratio: '16/9',
    label:
      'A cleaner in a high-visibility vest sweeping fine dust from the concrete floor of a newly finished, unfurnished interior',
    src: heroImg,
  },
};

// §2 Definition. Answer-target block, and it has to do double duty: define
// the term and separate the three stages, because "builders clean" means a
// different thing to a builder, a project manager and a homeowner.
const definitionBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'A builders clean is the cleaning of a construction site as the work finishes: removing dust, debris, adhesive, plaster and stickers so the building can be inspected, handed over and lived in. TLB Cleaning does builders cleans and final detail cleans across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed, for new builds, renovations, fitouts and unit developments.',
  'It is really three jobs at three different moments, and quotes get compared wrongly because people mean different ones by the same phrase. A progress clean keeps a working site safe and passable. A builders clean happens once the trades are out and takes the building from construction-dirty to visibly clean. A final detail clean is the pass after that, where a building goes from clean to handover ready. Which ones you need changes the price enormously, so it is the first thing worth settling.',
];

// §3 Silica. `warning`, and THE MOST SERIOUS WARNING ON THIS SITE.
//
// Placed before the scope, same reasoning as the clinical waste callout on
// the medical page: what a contractor refuses to do badly is the thing a
// competent site supervisor screens for first.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and take this one seriously:
//   - Respirable crystalline silica is generated by cutting, grinding and
//     sweeping concrete, brick, tile, render and engineered stone, and dry
//     sweeping is a recognised way of putting settled dust back into the air.
//     Correct.
//   - Australia banned the manufacture, supply and installation of
//     engineered stone benchtops from 1 July 2024. Correct as at this build,
//     and the reason the paragraph names engineered stone.
//   - Safe Work Australia and the state regulators set exposure standards and
//     control requirements. Correct. NO EXPOSURE FIGURE IS QUOTED on purpose:
//     the workplace exposure standard for respirable crystalline silica has
//     been reduced within recent memory and a stale number here would be
//     both wrong and dangerous.
//   - The brand foundation document (§3.3) names Safe Work Australia as an
//     approved authoritative source, so a link belongs here at launch.
const silicaCallout = {
  heading: 'Dry sweeping construction dust is not cleaning. It is exposure.',
  body: [
    'The dust on a new build is not house dust. Cutting, grinding and even sweeping concrete, brick, tile, render and engineered stone puts respirable crystalline silica into the air, and it is fine enough to stay there for hours and small enough to reach the deepest part of the lung. It causes silicosis, and silicosis does not get better. Engineered stone benchtops were banned outright in Australia from 1 July 2024 because of it.',
    'So a cleaner who turns up to a builders clean with a broom and a domestic vacuum is not being cheap, they are generating an exposure on your site and putting it in your incident history rather than theirs. The controls are not exotic: wet methods where the surface allows, an H-class vacuum with the right filtration rather than a shop vac, and respiratory protection worn properly. Ask any cleaner quoting your site which of those three they are bringing.',
  ],
};

// §4 The three stages. `ServiceBlocks variant="slider"` — THE ONLY USE OF
// THIS VARIANT IN THE CLUSTER, and it is here because these three genuinely
// happen in order. Prev/next through a sequence is what the variant is for
// (see SECTIONS.md: navigation, not selection — the medical page's three
// settings are a selection and use `highlight` instead).
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Two of the three carry real brackets, and both are equipment or licensing
// questions rather than copy questions.
const stageItems = [
  {
    image: {
      ratio: '16/9',
      label:
        'A cleaner in a high-visibility vest sweeping fine dust from the concrete floor of a newly finished, unfurnished interior',
      src: heroImg,
    },
    title: 'Stage one: progress clean, while the trades are still in',
    description:
      // ⚠️ Real bracket. Builders routinely expect the cleaner to remove
      // rubbish, and that needs a vehicle, a tip account and a price.
      'Offcuts, packaging and general debris cleared so the site stays passable and nobody trips over the last trade’s mess. Floors kept clear for the trades who need a clean substrate. Amenities and the site office cleaned, which is the part everyone skips and everyone complains about. Waste removal from site is [included / quoted separately, and requires a tip fee. Confirm].',
    cta: walkthroughCta,
  },
  {
    image: {
      ratio: '4/3',
      label:
        'A cleaner in a high-visibility vest detailing plaster dust off a new aluminium window frame in an unfurnished new build',
      src: windowsImg,
    },
    title: 'Stage two: the builders clean, once the trades are out',
    description:
      // ⚠️ Real bracket, and it is the equipment question a builder asks.
      'The heavy pass. Plaster dust off every horizontal surface including the tops of doors, architraves and skirtings. Paint spots, adhesive, silicone smears and stickers off glass, tiles and tapware. Window frames and tracks vacuumed out. Cupboards and drawers cleaned inside. Floors vacuumed then washed, not swept. Done with [an H-class vacuum. Confirm TLB owns one] rather than a broom, for the reason in the block above.',
    cta: walkthroughCta,
  },
  {
    image: {
      ratio: '4/3',
      label:
        'A cleaner in a high-visibility vest polishing the brushed-metal tapware of a brand new bathroom during a final detail clean',
      src: bathroomImg,
    },
    title: 'Stage three: the final detail clean, before handover',
    description:
      'The pass that decides how the building feels on the day. Glass and mirrors polished streak free, tapware and stainless finished, appliance protective film removed, splashbacks and grout lines detailed, light fittings and switch plates wiped, and the dust that settled out of the air overnight taken off again. Timed to the morning of handover where the program allows, because dust keeps landing.',
    cta: walkthroughCta,
  },
];

// §5 What decides the price. `ContentGrid` with `feature` cells. Included
// because this is the premises type where a quote most often turns out to
// mean something different from what the builder assumed, and naming the
// variables is more useful than a number we do not have.
const priceDriverBlocks = [
  {
    type: 'feature' as const,
    icon: 'chart-pie' as const,
    heading: 'Which stages you actually want',
    body: [
      'One builders clean and one detail clean is the normal pair. A quote for one, compared against a quote for both, looks like one cleaner is half the price of the other. Settle the stages before you compare anything.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'feature' as const,
    icon: 'idea' as const,
    heading: 'How much glass there is',
    body: [
      'Glass is the slowest surface on a new build and the one a handover is judged on. A house with a wall of stacker doors takes hours longer than the same floor area in solid wall, and adhesive residue on new glass takes longer again.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'feature' as const,
    icon: 'target' as const,
    heading: 'What the trades left behind',
    body: [
      'A site handed over swept and free of rubbish costs less to clean than one with offcuts in every room. Paint overspray, silicone smears and render splatter on tile are the three that turn a day into two.',
    ],
  },
  {
    type: 'feature' as const,
    icon: 'key' as const,
    heading: 'Whether the date can move',
    body: [
      'A builders clean booked to a fixed handover date, on a weekend, at short notice, is a different price from one with a week of float either side. Penalty rates under the Cleaning Services Award are real and they appear on the quote rather than in the fine print.',
    ],
  },
];

// §6 "Worth knowing". The lever on this page is the defect list: a builders
// clean is the only trade whose work is judged by someone actively looking
// for faults, and dust is what gets written up.
const worthKnowingBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'The reason a builders clean is worth doing properly has nothing to do with how it looks and everything to do with the defect list. At handover somebody walks the building specifically looking for things to write down. Dust in a window track, adhesive on a shower screen, plaster on the top of a door, a sticker still on an appliance: none of those are building defects, but they all get written up as ones, and then a supervisor spends a fortnight closing items that a cleaner should have closed in an afternoon.',
  'The other reason is that dust keeps arriving. A building that has been sealed for a week still has fine dust settling out of the air, off the ceiling and out of the ducts. That is why the final detail clean is a separate stage rather than the same clean done more carefully, and why timing it close to handover is worth more than doing it thoroughly a fortnight early. We book to your program and we will tell you honestly if the date you have picked is the wrong one.',
];

// §7 Related pages. Real estate and end of lease are appended as extras: a
// builder finishing a spec home and an agent presenting it are the same
// handover from two sides, and the reader is often both.
const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/construction-site/', [
  { label: 'Real estate cleaners', href: '/real-estate-cleaning/' },
  { label: 'End of lease and bond cleaning', href: '/end-of-lease-cleaning/' },
]);

// §8 Social proof. Bracketed [TBC] briefing slots, NOT real testimonials.
const testimonials = [
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote from a builder or site supervisor about the handover date being hit. Nothing else on this page matters to that reader as much as the date. Named business and role, with written permission.]',
    name: '[Builder, TBC]',
    role: '[Role, for example site supervisor, TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'TLB builders clean client' },
  },
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote about the defect list specifically, from a supervisor who has had cleaning items written up on a previous job. That is §6’s argument in a client’s own words.]',
    name: '[Business name, TBC]',
    role: '[Role, TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'Site supervisor, TLB client' },
  },
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote from a renovation client rather than a commercial builder, since a homeowner moving back into their own renovated house is the other half of this page’s audience and wants a different thing.]',
    name: '[Client name, TBC]',
    role: '[Renovation client, TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'Renovation client, TLB builders clean' },
  },
];

// §9 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — a builder, supervisor, project
// manager or a homeowner mid-renovation.
const brandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Are your cleaners inducted to work on site?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ THE page's gate, along with the H-class vacuum. See the file
      // header. Without white cards, no builder can lawfully let TLB on site.
      'Any cleaner attending a construction site needs a general construction induction card, the white card, plus your own site induction, and they need to sign in and out like every other trade. [Whether TLB holds a current white card for every cleaner who would attend, and whether TLB can provide a SWMS for the work. Confirm before this page ships.] Ask us for the cards and the SWMS before we come on site, not after.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you work to our handover date?',
    answer:
      'Yes, and that is usually the whole brief. Tell us the handover date and we work backwards: the builders clean once the trades are genuinely out, then the final detail clean as close to the morning of handover as your program allows. If the date moves, tell us as early as you can and we will move with it where we have the capacity.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you take the rubbish away?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ Same bracket as §4 stage one.
      'Debris and packaging get cleared and bagged as part of the clean. Taking it off site is [included / a separate line with a tip fee. Confirm], because it needs a vehicle and a disposal cost rather than just labour. Bins on site make it cheaper for everyone, so if there is still a skip when we arrive, say so.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What about the windows?',
    answer:
      'Internal glass, frames and tracks are part of a builders clean, and we detail adhesive, paint and render off glass rather than scraping at it. External glass at ground level is included. Anything above ground level needs its own plan and pricing, and depending on the building that means a different licence and equipment than a cleaning team carries. We will tell you which it is at the walkthrough rather than after.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you do renovations, not just new builds?',
    answer:
      'Yes, and it is a different job. A renovation is dirtier per square metre than a new build because the dust travels into a house that is already furnished and lived in, so the clean extends past the work area into the rooms nobody touched. If you are moving back into your own renovated house, that is the version of this we do most.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I get in touch?',
    answer:
      'We walk the site, work out which stages you actually need, and tell you which ones you do not. Then you get a written scope per stage with a price against each and the date each one would happen. No obligation. If the program is not settled yet, we will hold an indicative date rather than pretend it is booked.',
  },
];

// §10 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. Checkable claims:
//   - A general construction induction card (white card) is required to carry
//     out construction work in NSW. Correct.
//   - Dry sweeping is a recognised silica exposure risk and wet methods plus
//     H-class vacuums are the standard controls. Correct; no exposure figure
//     quoted, on purpose.
//   - "Builders clean" has no legal or standard definition, which is why the
//     answer below tells the reader to define it in writing. Correct and
//     genuinely useful.
//   - No dollar figure anywhere, same as every sibling page.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'How much does a builders clean cost?',
    answer:
      // ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.
      'It is priced per job rather than per hour, against a written scope, and the number moves with which stages you need, how much glass there is, how much the trades left behind and whether the date is fixed. Floor area on its own is a poor predictor, which is why a phone quote for a builders clean is usually wrong. [TLB’s pricing basis. Confirm.]',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is included in a builders clean?',
    answer:
      'There is no standard definition, which is exactly why it should be written down. A normal scope covers dust removal from all surfaces including the tops of doors and architraves, adhesive stickers silicone and paint spots off glass tiles and tapware, window frames and tracks vacuumed, cupboards cleaned inside, and floors vacuumed then washed. If a quote just says "builders clean", ask for that list.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between a builders clean and a final clean?',
    answer:
      'The builders clean is the heavy pass that takes a building from construction-dirty to clean, done once the trades are out. The final clean, or detail clean, is the pass after it that takes the building from clean to handover ready, and it exists because fine dust keeps settling for days. Doing only the first leaves dust on every sill on handover morning. Doing only the second costs more, because the second pass is not designed to shift plaster.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do construction cleaners need a white card?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Anyone carrying out construction work on a site in New South Wales needs a general construction induction card, commonly called a white card, and they also need the site-specific induction for that particular site. It is the principal contractor who carries the duty to make sure everyone on site is inducted, so a cleaner without one is the builder’s problem as much as the cleaner’s.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is construction dust dangerous?',
    answer:
      'Because of what is in it. Concrete, brick, tile, render and engineered stone contain crystalline silica, and cutting, grinding or dry sweeping them produces respirable particles fine enough to reach deep into the lung and cause silicosis, which is irreversible. Dry sweeping is one of the worst methods because it re-suspends dust that had already settled. Wet methods, H-class vacuum extraction and properly fitted respiratory protection are the controls that actually work.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When should the builders clean be booked?',
    answer:
      'Once the trades are genuinely finished, not when they are nearly finished. A builders clean done while one trade is still cutting is money spent twice, and it is the single most common scheduling mistake on a build. Book the heavy pass for after the last trade leaves, and the detail clean as close to handover as the program allows.',
  },
];

// §11 Closing CTA.
const closingCta = {
  kicker: 'Builders clean and construction site cleaning',
  heading: 'Tell us your handover date.',
  lead: 'We will walk the site, work out which stages you actually need, and price each one separately. If the date you have picked is the wrong one, we will say so.',
  cta: walkthroughCta,
};

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="full-width-photo"
      kicker={hero.kicker}
      headingLines={hero.headingLines}
      lead={hero.lead}
      cta={hero.cta}
      image={hero.image}
    />

    <Backdrop image={definitionBgImg} tint="muted" opacity={75}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock theme="muted" align="left" body={definitionBody} />
    </Backdrop>

    {/* §3 — before the scope. The most serious warning on the site, and the
        thing a competent supervisor screens a cleaner for first. */}
    <Callout tone="warning" heading={silicaCallout.heading} body={silicaCallout.body} />

    {/* §4 — the only `slider` in the cluster. Three stages that genuinely
        happen in order, so navigation through a sequence rather than a
        selection between alternatives. See SECTIONS.md on the distinction. */}
    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="slider"
      heading="Three cleans, in this order"
      lead="Most quotes get compared wrongly because two people mean different stages by the same phrase. Settle which of these you want first."
      items={stageItems}
    />

    {/* The detail-clean frame, given a band of its own because it is the one
        photo on the page showing the OUTCOME rather than the work, and the
        outcome is what a handover is judged on.

        ⚠️ Source is 4:3 in a 16/9 box, so `cover` crops top and bottom.
        Checked: the vanity, the tapware and the screen all survive it. */}
    <ImageBand
      ratio="16/9"
      label="A brand new bathroom during the final detail clean, tapware polished and the glass screen finished"
      caption="The final detail clean. This is the pass that decides what the defect list looks like."
      src={bathroomImg}
    />

    <ContentGrid
      heading="What actually decides the price"
      lead="Four variables, none of which is floor area on its own."
      columns={2}
      blocks={priceDriverBlocks}
    />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading="Cleaning items on a defect list are still your problem"
      body={worthKnowingBody}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading="Other premises we clean"
      subheading="Commercial cleaning for every kind of site across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed."
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={[]}
    />

    <TestimonialCarousel heading="What builders say" testimonials={testimonials} uniform />

    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we clean"
      subheading="Across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed."
      groups={nswLocationGroups}
      note={["If a town isn't listed, ask us anyway."]}
    />

    <Faq heading="Frequently Asked Questions" items={brandedFaqItems} />
    <Faq heading="Builders Clean FAQs" items={nonBrandedFaqItems} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={closingCta.kicker}
      heading={closingCta.heading}
      lead={closingCta.lead}
      cta={closingCta.cta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
